{\sffamily\bfseries\fontsize{8.4}{10}\selectfont\color{teal}ABSTRACT}\par\vspace{.6mm}
\fontsize{8.9}{10.9}\selectfont\RaggedRight
AirDash integrates public environmental feeds into a warning-system prototype for Thailand and neighbouring countries. This developer-led audit evaluates data coverage, collection reliability, retrospective forecast comparisons and corroborating optical signals; it does not test institutional coordination, public uptake or health effects. Over {{a1.window.days|int}} days (17 July--3 November 2026), {{a2.runs_total|int}} scheduled pulls were logged, {{a2.success_overall|pct1}} as successful, although two token-gated feeds produced no readings. Feed timestamps reached the collector a median of {{a3.pcd_lag_min.median|f0}} minutes after their labelled hour; notification delivery could not be verified from the available broadcast logs. In the observed ground-feed sample, {{a9.stations_per_province.bangkok_share|pct0}} of stations were in Bangkok and three provinces had none. Two official products assigned different bands on {{derived.category_differ|pct0}} of paired province-days, mainly between the two cleanest bands; their spatial support and averaging semantics differ. {{derived.outside_cell_share|pct0}} of fire cell-days in the specified mainland box were outside Thailand, which does not quantify their contribution to Thai pollution. Only {{a4.warnings_unique.any_station_gt37_5|int}} of {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} warned province-days exceeded 37.5~\pmu{} at any station's derived daily mean. Persistence outperformed a retrospective CAMS correction variant; issue-time data availability was not reconstructed. Cameras and airport visibility offered weak corroboration in this predominantly wet-season record. We release summary inputs, corrected figures and typesetting code, identify limits to independent reproduction, and propose prospective tests requiring an implementation freeze before evaluation.
\par\vspace{1.4mm}
