\section{Introduction}
Every dry season the north of Thailand disappears under smoke. The cost is counted in clinics. In the upper north, same-day \pmten{} was associated with more outpatient visits for chronic lower respiratory disease (incidence rate ratio 1.020, 95\% CI 1.012--1.028) \citep{mueller2020bb}; all-cause mortality was associated with a 0.9\% increase for every 10~\pmu{} of vegetation-fire \pmtf{} \citep{uttajug2024fire}; and in haze periods the fatality and re-admission rates for COPD rose significantly \citep{sapbamrer2024copd}. Globally, smoke from landscape fires is estimated to kill about 339,000 people a year, 110,000 of them in Southeast Asia \citep{johnston2012lfs}.

The smoke is also not only Thai. A source-apportionment simulation of 2019 for the upper north puts Myanmar as the main contributor in the west and centre and domestic burning in the east at the peak of the season \citep{chantaraprachoom2024nation}. Burma alone accounted for 37\% of the hotspots in Southeast Asia in March in one analysis \citep{amnuaylojaroen2020lrt}. Across Southeast Asia, an estimated 23\% of the roughly 900 thousand premature deaths a year from air pollution come from transboundary air, with impacts especially adverse in border regions \citep{gu2024seasource}. Origin and destination are different places, run by different governments.

Southeast Asia has a treaty for this. The ASEAN Agreement on Transboundary Haze Pollution was signed in 2002 \citep{aathp2002text}; Indonesia, the last of the ten members, deposited its instrument in January 2015 \citep{aseanhub_indonesia_ratification,aathp_portal}. The agreement is a legally binding treaty; scholars criticise its limited enforcement and emphasis on sovereignty and non-intervention \citep{heilmann2015athp,hurley2020delayed}, and two ASEAN roadmaps have followed, for 2016--2020 and for 2023--2030 \citep{haze_roadmap1,haze_roadmap2}. Inside Thailand, policy reviews describe many agencies with different mandates and little integration across sectors \citep{sei2021thailand}, and a structural problem of silo culture \citep{tdri2023pm25}; one analysis argues that fragmentation and protected economic interests have compromised the transboundary governance of the air \citep{marks2022transbdry}. We do not try to settle the diplomacy here. We take it as given that neither the treaty nor the agencies will change before the next burning season, and ask what can be built in the meantime.

We name three gaps. The first is \emph{coordination}. The data that matter are public, but they sit in separate systems: ground stations at one agency, a satellite--ground fusion at another, burn scars at a research institute, weather at a third, and fires at NASA. The second is \emph{responsiveness}. A number on a dashboard is not a response. Alerts can change behaviour but they also fatigue, the evidence on health outcomes is mixed, and localised, actionable information is likely to help \citep{saberian2017alerts,janjua2021cochrane,riley2021communicate}. The third is the \emph{end-to-end chain}. Nobody owns the path from the field where the fire starts, through the air that carries it, to the person it reaches.

\textbf{Research question.} Can a small team integrate published environmental feeds into a usable warning-system prototype, and what limitations emerge from an audit of its initial operational record? Agency coordination and behaviour change are motivations, not outcomes measured here.

\textbf{Contributions.} (i)~An open design and implementation of the chain (Section~\ref{sec:system}). (ii)~A quantitative audit of the integrated record: {{a1.window.days|int}} days, {{a1.stations_pm25|int}} ground stations and {{a2.runs_total|int}} pulls (Section~\ref{sec:results}). (iii)~The system's failures, reported with the same weight as its successes. (iv)~A proposed prospective evaluation plan, requiring executable protocols and author approval before the evaluation window (Section~\ref{sec:tests}).

\textbf{Scope.} We have not obtained a multi-year, machine-readable ground record. The ground record here begins on 17 July 2026 and ends on 3 November 2026, before our proposed evaluation window of 1 December to 30 April; actual burning seasons vary geographically and meteorologically. The multi-year argument rests on the literature and on three seasons of burn-scar maps (2024--2026). What follows is therefore a systems paper with an early-season audit, not a trend analysis. Where the data cannot carry a claim, we say so.

\section{Background}
\textbf{Where the smoke comes from.} A 2003--2014 modelling study attributed nearly all visibility events below 7~km in its Southeast Asian sample to fire aerosols, and 99\% of simulated fire-produced \pmtf{} in Bangkok \citep{lee2017lowvis}. The region's aerosol system is among the most complex in the world and its satellite aerosol products diverge widely \citep{reid2013review}; field campaigns document the biomass-burning aerosol near its sources and far downwind \citep{lin2013overview}. El Ni\~no strongly modulates burning in the Maritime Continent \citep{reid2012mc,marlier2013elnino}, but Cambodia, Myanmar and Thailand respond far less: hotspot severity rose by under 25\% in El Ni\~no, positive-dipole or combined years, against more than 72\% in Kalimantan in the same comparison \citep{nurdiati2022enso}. One global chemistry model underestimated the aerosol burden, most strongly early in the burning season \citep{marvin2024bbuncert}.

\textbf{What can be measured.} Regulatory stations remain the reference. Low-cost optical sensors fill the gaps but read high without calibration, by about 40\% in one large US evaluation \citep{barkjohn2021purpleair}, and their fitness for purpose depends on the calibration and on the aerosol \citep{giordano2021lowcost,morawska2018lowcost,castell2017lowcost}. More than 1,500 such sensors are installed in Thailand \citep{dejchanchaiwong2023lcs}; DustBoy, run from Chiang Mai University, is one such network \citep{cmuccdc}. From space, VIIRS detects active fires at 375~m \citep{schroeder2014viirs} through NASA's FIRMS service \citep{davies2009firms,nasa_firms}, and AERONET gives the column aerosol optical depth and the \AA ngstr\"om exponent, a qualitative indicator of particle size \citep{holben1998aeronet,giles2019aeronetv3,schuster2006angstrom,angstrom1929dust}. Thai agencies publish a fire layer \citep{gistda_fire} and a burn-scar monitoring site \citep{tamroypao}, and academic work maps burned area from Sentinel-2 imagery \citep{suwanprasit2024obia}.

\textbf{What can be forecast.} CAMS, the Copernicus atmosphere service, is the free workhorse; its reanalysis assimilates satellite aerosol retrievals \citep{inness2019cams}. Its \pmtf{} forecast skill is modest and uneven: weaker over Malaysia than elsewhere \citep{tan2023camsmalaysia}, strongly lead-dependent in China \citep{wu2020camsforecast}, and biased in different directions in different regions \citep{ali2022camsmerra}. Model output statistics, regressing the model onto local observations, is the classical remedy \citep{glahn1972mos}. Trajectory models such as HYSPLIT trace where an air parcel came from \citep{stein2015hysplit}.

\textbf{What the eye and the camera can see.} Horizontal visibility follows the Koschmieder relation between extinction and range \citep{koschmieder1924}, and visibility-derived extinction tracks \pmtf{} well in polluted regions but poorly in the cleanest \citep{ye2023visibility}. Haze can be estimated from a single image \citep{he2011dcp}, and camera-derived extinction has correlated with \pmtf{} at $R^2=0.7$ over a limited concentration range \citep{shin2022camera}. A hybrid CNN--LSTM on Bangkok photographs improved on plain CNNs \citep{laohakiat2023bkk}, and a model that joins surveillance images to numerical data forecast \pmtf{} in Shanghai well \citep{wu2024surveillance}. In Chiang Mai, however, trained staff estimating the visual range reached 79\% accuracy for \pmten{} but only 26\% for \pmtf{} \citep{theerawasttanasiri2024visual}.

\textbf{Warning people.} Warnings change what people do, and they fatigue. A Sydney study found alerts cut cycling by 14--35\% and also found evidence of alert fatigue \citep{saberian2017alerts}; English data show avoidance behaviour for children's asthma \citep{janke2014avoidance}; a Cochrane review found the evidence on health-care use conflicting \citep{janjua2021cochrane}; and a review of 37 communication studies found actionable, localised information likely to help \citep{riley2021communicate}. A review of citizen-science monitoring found little research on how its data feed policy \citep{mahajan2022citizen}.

\section{The system}\label{sec:system}
\subsection{Design rules}
We wrote five rules and have tried to keep them; Table~\ref{tab:incidents} and Section~\ref{sec:results} show where we did not.
\textbf{Published interfaces first.} Integrate published feeds subject to their access conditions, attribution, tokens and quotas; public availability does not imply unrestricted reuse. These integrations did not require bespoke inter-agency data-sharing agreements. Where a stream needs a free token, the system is meant to say so rather than fail quietly.
\textbf{Provenance on every number.} Each value carries its source, its observation time and its age; a number without a time is not shown.
\textbf{Corroborate, never substitute.} Cameras, airport visibility and satellite aerosol may raise a flag or add a reason to the verdict; they never produce a \pmu{} figure.
\textbf{Fail closed, and say so.} Inbound webhooks are refused until their secrets are configured. ``Unknown'' is never reported as ``fine''.
\textbf{One laptop.} The server is Node.js with {{a10.system.npm_dependencies|int}} package dependencies and one SQLite file; anyone can run it. The code is {{a10.system.loc_server|int}} lines of server, {{a10.system.loc_frontend_js|int}} of browser JavaScript and {{a10.system.loc_tests|int}} of tests in {{a10.system.test_files|int}} files, with {{a10.system.api_routes|int}} API routes, over {{a10.system.commits|int}} commits since {{a10.system.first_commit}}, written with AI coding assistants under the direction of one author.

\subsection{The chain}
Figure~\ref{fig:chain} shows the path from a fire to a phone. Table~\ref{tab:census} lists what the system reads.

\emph{Ground and fusion.} The PCD-hosted feed supplied timestamped snapshots from {{a1.stations_pm25|int}} \pmtf{} stations; the Geo-Informatics and Space Technology Development Agency (GISTDA) publishes a satellite--ground fusion for all 77 provinces. Both are polled and stored with their own observation times.

\emph{Forecast and its correction.} CAMS \pmtf{} forecasts arrive through Open-Meteo for each province. They read low against Thai ground stations, so the system applies a province-specific ratio learned over the trailing 14 days, clamped to 0.5--3, and falls back to the national ratio when a province has fewer than five paired days. This is a simple form of model output statistics \citep{glahn1972mos}. A ratio learned on clean days overshoots on dirty ones, so it applies only up to the mean CAMS level it was learned on; beyond that, the calibrated absolute offset is added instead.

\emph{Where the smoke is coming from.} VIIRS detections from three satellites (Suomi-NPP, NOAA-20, NOAA-21) are gridded to 0.25$^\circ$ cells. For each province the system sums the fire radiative power of cells within 500~km and within $\pm45^\circ$ of the forecast wind-from direction, weighted by $1/(1+d/150)$ for distance $d$ in km, and grades the resulting proxy at 20, 150 and 600 index units. Missing or zero radiative power receives a unit fallback in the replay, so this index is not a calibrated MW total. This is a transport indicator, not a concentration forecast: it says where smoke can come from, not how much will arrive.

\emph{The verdict.} A province's Air Watch Score is $0.40$ ground \pmtf{} + $0.10$ other pollutants + $0.15$ trend + $0.20$ forecast + $0.15$ stagnation, anchored to the Thai air quality index breakpoints, whose 24-hour \pmtf{} values are 15, 25, 37.5 and 75~\pmu{} \citep{pcd2023aqi}. Thailand's 24-hour standard has been 37.5~\pmu{} since 1 June 2023 \citep{nec2022pm25std}, which equals the WHO interim target~3; the WHO guideline is 15 \citep{who2021aqg}.

\emph{Corroboration.} Public cameras (about 1,000 in the catalogue, drawn from GISTDA, iTIC, the Department of Water Resources and a volunteer aggregator) are judged only against their own clear-day baseline at a similar hour. A province whose cameras look hazier than usual gets a reason added to its verdict; if fog is likelier (relative humidity $\ge90\%$ or an airport report of mist) the reason says so. Airport visibility from METAR reports is read through the Koschmieder relation \citep{koschmieder1924}. In exactly one case do cameras move a band: no ground sensor is reporting and haze is widespread without fog, which raises the province to at least WATCH.

\emph{The push.} Alerts are raised by rule (level crossed, forecast above the line, all-clear, rain washout) and fanned out to Telegram and LINE subscribers. Telegram is the main channel because the free LINE tier is capped at 300 messages a month, which one haze episode would exhaust. A gap of 30 minutes between broadcasts and of three hours per subscriber keeps a volatile hour from becoming a flood. Both webhooks verify who is speaking.

\subsection{Built to survive a season}
The system runs on one laptop behind a named tunnel and a static front end. Table~\ref{tab:incidents} lists what broke between July and October and what was changed. Each row is a rule the system now enforces rather than a lesson learned.

\section{Data and methods}\label{sec:methods}
\textbf{Window.} All analyses are frozen at 00:00 on 4 November 2026 (Bangkok time). Ground and reliability records cover 17 July to 3 November ({{a1.window.days|int}} days, {{a1.window.hours|int}} hours). Fire detections cover 21 October to 3 November (14 days); camera frames 28 September to 3 November; airport reports 11 October to 3 November; the alert and Telegram log 28 September to 3 November; the PCD reading lag from 7 August; burn scars for 16 months between January 2024 and April 2026 (62 provinces); forecast pairs 7 August to 3 November.

\textbf{Ground record.} Values are snapshots of the PCD-hosted feed. Their underlying averaging period was not retained, so hourly timestamps do not establish independent one-hour exposures. A station-day needs at least 18 snapshots; a province-day averages those station-day means. These derived means are compared with guideline thresholds, not certified as official regulatory exceedances. Values at or above 500~\pmu{} are excluded by a screening rule ({{a1.pm25_fault_values|int}} values at {{a1.pm25_fault_stations|int}} stations; raw maximum {{a1.pm25_raw_max|int}}). Extreme concentrations can be real; this threshold does not diagnose instrument faults, and sensitivity to the exclusion has not been established. An exceedance is labelled isolated when at least two stations within 50~km reported that day and their median was under half its value; this cannot distinguish a local source from measurement error. ``Upper north'' means Chiang Mai, Chiang Rai, Lampang, Lamphun, Mae Hong Son, Nan, Phayao and Phrae.

\textbf{Intervals.} Where the unit of analysis repeats (frames per camera and hours per airport; fire comparisons by calendar day) we use a 95\% percentile interval from a bootstrap that resamples whole clusters \citep{efron1979bootstrap}, with 400 to 2,000 resamples depending on cost. Simple proportions over station-days, detections, alerts and warned province-days carry Wilson 95\% intervals \citep{wilson1927,brown2001binomial} that treat the observations as independent; these are optimistic where events cluster in time, and we say so where it matters. Rank correlations are Spearman's $\rho$ \citep{spearman1904}; agreement between category assignments is Cohen's $\kappa$.

\textbf{Forecast verification.} For lead $k$, the target is a derived province-day mean on day $D$ and the forecast is the first selected morning issue on local day $D-k$. This is a retrospective calendar-day holdout variant, not a reconstructed operational replay. Correction ratios use completed daily means through $I-1$, and persistence uses the previous day's mean; their availability at issue time was not checked using receipt timestamps. The replay differs from production's issue aggregation and treatment of partial current-day observations. Forecast intervals resample provinces, not calendar days, and do not account for shared daily shocks or serial dependence. They are exploratory; an as-of replay and day/block sensitivity are required before operational skill claims. Events use POD, false-alarm ratio and CSI \citep{jolliffe2012forecastverification,wilks2019stats}.

\textbf{Smoke transport replay.} The upwind indicator is re-implemented from the production code (500~km radius, $\pm45^\circ$ sector, distance weights, 0.25$^\circ$ cells, a two-day look-back, cells within 15~km counted whatever the wind) and restricted to the box the production code uses, 4--29$^\circ$N and 92--110$^\circ$E. It is evaluated with the wind forecast issued on the morning of each day (today's, where production prefers tomorrow's). Each province's next-day \pmtf{} and log weighted source index are centred on the province's own mean, to remove geography, and then on the day's mean across provinces, to remove everything that changes for all provinces together, such as the fortnight-long fall in fire counts. Provinces share the weather on a given day, so the interval resamples days, of which only {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.days|int}} have a next-day record.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} Data-derived template statistics are filled from frozen JSON summaries; missing or non-finite values stop the build. The supplement includes these summaries, selected extracts, figure PDFs, manuscript source and build code, sufficient to rebuild the typeset PDF with Python and LaTeX. It does not release the complete databases or operational logs: several extraction scripts still require them, so independent end-to-end statistical reproduction is not yet possible. Operational analysis uses aggregate alert/broadcast timestamps and counts, not subscriber identifiers, subscriber-authored message contents or behaviour.

