\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
\subsection{The network we inherited}
The observed PCD-hosted feed contained \pmtf{} snapshots from {{a1.stations_pm25|int}} stations in {{a1.provinces_with_pm25_station|int}} of 77 provinces (Fig.~\ref{fig:coord}a). Bangkok holds {{a9.stations_per_province.bangkok_count|int}} of them, {{a9.stations_per_province.bangkok_share|pct0}} of the network. Three provinces (Phetchaburi, Phang Nga and Ranong) have none in this feed sample, and {{a9.stations_per_province.with_exactly_1|int}} have exactly one. The eight upper-north provinces, where the dust season does its worst, share {{derived.upper_north_stations|int}} stations. Only {{a1.stations_pm10|int}} stations also report \pmten{}, in {{a1.provinces_with_pm10_station|int}} provinces, so size-fraction comparisons are available in fewer than half the provinces; these alone cannot identify smoke, dust or chemical composition. Of the {{a1.stations_with_daily_data|int}} stations that produced at least one full station-day, {{a1.completeness.n_ge_90pct|int}} reported at least 90\% of their hours, and {{a1.completeness.n_lt_50pct|int}} reported fewer than half.

The record itself is quiet (Fig.~\ref{fig:season}). Across {{a1.station_days|int}} station-days, {{a1.exceed_gt_who15.share|pct0}} exceeded the numerical WHO 24-hour benchmark of 15~\pmu{}, but only {{a1.exceed_gt_thai37_5.k|int}} ({{a1.exceed_gt_thai37_5.share|pct2}}; 95\% CI {{a1.exceed_gt_thai37_5.ci[0] .. a1.exceed_gt_thai37_5.ci[1]|pct2}}) exceeded the numerical Thai 24-hour benchmark of 37.5, and {{a1.exceed_gt_75.k|int}} exceeded 75. The monthly mean of station-days was {{a1.by_month.2026-07.mean|f1}} in July (from the 17th), {{a1.by_month.2026-08.mean|f1}} in August, {{a1.by_month.2026-09.mean|f1}} in September and {{a1.by_month.2026-10.mean|f1}}~\pmu{} in October, with a station mix that changes from month to month. The worst day was 9 October, when the highest station in each of {{a1.worst_day_provinces_gt37_5[0][1]|int}} provinces crossed 37.5. Of the {{a1.exceed_gt_thai37_5.k|int}} exceeding station-days, {{a1.exceed_37_5_isolated|int}} were isolated spikes, {{a1.exceed_37_5_corroborated|int}} were not isolated, and {{a1.exceed_37_5_unverifiable|int}} had too few neighbours to tell. The largest value in the record, {{a1.top_station_days[0].pm25|f1}}~\pmu{} at one station in Chon Buri on 7 September, stood alone: the median of its {{derived.top_station_neighbors|int}} neighbours within 50~km was {{derived.top_station_neighbor_median|f1}}. That station supplied {{a1.pm25_fault_by_station.33t|int}} of the {{a1.pm25_fault_values|int}} screened-out values, and the highest hourly value that survived the screening filter was {{a1.pm25_clean_max|f0}}. We read this as a baseline. It is the clean air against which the burning season will be judged, and it is not evidence that the north is safe.

\subsection{Does the machinery hold?}
The collector made {{a2.runs_total|int}} scheduled pulls across {{a2.sources|int}} sources, and {{a2.failed_runs|int}} failed ({{derived.failed_share|pct1}}). It was up {{a2.collector_up_share|pct1}} of the window. Seven gaps longer than six hours add up to {{a2.collector_gaps_gt6h_hours|f0}} hours (Fig.~\ref{fig:clock}a). Two match entries in Table~\ref{tab:incidents}: {{a2.collector_gaps_gt6h[4].minutes|min2h}} hours on 26--27 September (the database incident) and {{a2.collector_gaps_gt6h[6].minutes|min2h}} hours on 24--25 October (the disk failure). The other five, the longest {{a2.collector_gaps_gt6h[3].minutes|min2h}} hours on 23--25 August, have no entry in our operating record and we cannot explain them. The newest successful PCD pull was no older than two hours {{a2.pm25_feed_fresh_share_2h|pct1}} of the time; the feed was stale for {{a2.pm25_feed_stale_hours|f0}} hours in {{derived.stale_episodes_n|int}} episodes.

The timestamp comparisons describe separate processes (Fig.~\ref{fig:clock}b--d). A PCD snapshot was first seen a median of {{a3.pcd_lag_min.median|f0}} minutes after its labelled hour (95th percentile {{a3.pcd_lag_min.p95|f0}}). For {{a3.alert_to_telegram.alerts|int}} severe alerts, the next logged broadcast occurred a median of {{a3.alert_to_telegram.median_min|min2s}} seconds later. This is only a next-broadcast timing proxy: alert membership in that batch and successful recipient acceptance were not linked. It cannot establish alert hand-off, delivery, or end-to-end latency. We withdraw the simulated combined-hop latency from the earlier version because independent random pairing does not reconstruct a real alert chain. VIIRS detections were first seen a median of {{a3.firms_lag_min.median|f0}} minutes after overpass, with a tenth exceeding {{a3.firms_lag_min.p90|min2h}} hours. Six-hour polling contributes to this lag, alongside upstream publication and availability; their contributions were not separated. The interface should therefore display observation age rather than imply a live fire picture.

Success is not delivery. Five sources wrote no new readings in {{a1.window.days|int}} days. Three of them (the weather relay, camera health and the flood sister system) relay into a key--value store or other tables by design. The other two, satellite rain (IMERG; {{census.rows[6].pulls|int}} pulls) and DustBoy ({{census.rows[13].pulls|int}} pulls), are gated by a free token that nobody had registered; their adapters skip quietly when it is absent, and both logged 100\% success. Together they are {{derived.token_gated_share|pct0}} of all pulls, so the headline success rate flatters the system. The bureaucracy we set out to bypass came back at the smallest scale, as a registration form, and the logs reported it as success.

\subsection{Two official answers to one question}
GISTDA's spatial fusion and sampled ground stations provide related but non-equivalent estimates; differing footprints, averaging periods and station mixes can produce disagreement. Over {{a9.paired_province_days|int}} paired province-days in {{a9.paired_provinces|int}} provinces they correlate ($\rho={{a9.pcd_vs_gistda.rho|f2}}$; 95\% CI {{a9.pcd_vs_gistda.rho_ci_cluster[0] .. a9.pcd_vs_gistda.rho_ci_cluster[1]|f2}}) but differ in level: GISTDA reads {{a9.pcd_vs_gistda.mean_diff_gistda_minus_pcd|f1}}~\pmu{} higher on average, with 95\% limits of agreement from {{a9.pcd_vs_gistda.loa[0]|f1}} to {{a9.pcd_vs_gistda.loa[1]|f1}} (Fig.~\ref{fig:coord}b). Sorted into Thai air-quality bands, they place a province in the same band on {{a9.category_agreement.same_category|pct0}} of days ($\kappa={{a9.category_agreement.kappa|f2}}$; weighted $\kappa={{derived.category_kappa_weighted|f2}}$), and GISTDA is the higher on {{a9.category_agreement.gistda_higher|pct0}} (Fig.~\ref{fig:coord}c). Of the {{derived.category_differ_n|int}} disagreements, {{derived.category_differ_cleanest_share|pct0}} lie between the two cleanest bands (15 or below, and 15--25), which GISTDA's offset largely explains. On the {{a9.above_25.0.n|int}} province-days compared, PCD alone put a province above 25 on {{derived.pcd_alone_25|int}}, GISTDA alone on {{derived.gistda_alone_25|int}}, and both on {{a9.above_25.0.both|int}}. Above 37.5, GISTDA never did so; PCD did on {{derived.pcd_only_above_37_5|int}} province-days. A citizen looking up the same province on two official sites will sometimes get two colours, usually adjacent ones. The two sources measure different things at different scales; we found no published rule for choosing between them.

\subsection{The forecast}
CAMS read low against the ground. At the same-day lead its mean bias was {{a4.leads.0.raw.bias|f1}}~\pmu{} over {{a4.leads.0.n_pairs|int}} province-days (mean absolute error, MAE, {{a4.leads.0.raw.mae|f1}}), and the pooled ratio of observation to model was {{a4.pooled_ratio_obs_over_cams|f2}}. The retrospective correction variant reduces descriptive error: MAE falls to {{a4.leads.0.cor.mae|f1}}, a change of {{a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_raw.mean|f1}} (95\% CI {{a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_raw.ci[0] .. a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_raw.ci[1]|f1}}) (Fig.~\ref{fig:forecast}a--c). Within this retrospective sample it improves on raw CAMS but does not beat persistence; issue-time availability and shared-day uncertainty remain unresolved. Carrying the last observed daily value forward has MAE {{a4.leads.0.pers.mae|f1}} at the same-day lead, so the corrected model is {{a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_persistence.mean|f2}} worse (CI {{a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_persistence.ci[0] .. a4.leads.0.mae_diff_cor_minus_persistence.ci[1]|f2}}). The gap narrows with lead (to {{a4.leads.1.mae_diff_cor_minus_persistence.mean|f2}} tomorrow and {{a4.leads.2.mae_diff_cor_minus_persistence.mean|f2}} the day after) but does not close. The correction leaves a residual bias of {{a4.leads.0.cor.bias|f1}}. The ratios behind it are large and uneven: the median province ratio was {{a4.province_ratio.median|f1}}, and {{a4.province_ratio.share_at_cap_3|pct0}} of province-days sat at the clamp of 3.

For events the picture is thin because the season was quiet. At the same-day lead the corrected forecast detected {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.cor.hits|int}} of {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.events_observed|int}} days above 25 (POD {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.cor.pod|f2}}, FAR {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.cor.far|f2}}, CSI {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.cor.csi|f2}}), against {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.pers.pod|f2}}, {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.pers.far|f2}} and {{a4.leads.0.events.25.0.pers.csi|f2}} for persistence, and none of the {{a4.leads.0.events.37.5.events_observed|int}} days above 37.5.

The system also logged forecast warnings; the alert table does not establish their dispatch. Of {{a4.warnings_summary.sent|int}} forecast warnings in {{a4.warnings_summary.provinces|int}} provinces, {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} distinct province-days could be checked against ground data. The median warned value was {{a4.warnings_unique.median_forecast|f0}}~\pmu{}; the median observed province mean was {{a4.warnings_unique.median_observed_province_mean|f0}}. Only {{a4.warnings_unique.any_station_gt37_5|int}} of {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} reached 37.5 at any station (Wilson 95\% CI {{a4.warnings_unique.wilson_any_station_gt37_5[0] .. a4.warnings_unique.wilson_any_station_gt37_5[1]|pct0}}) and {{a4.warnings_unique.province_mean_gt37_5|int}} in the province mean (Fig.~\ref{fig:forecast}d). {{derived.warned_overcalled|int}} of {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} over-called, though not wildly: {{a4.warnings_unique.province_mean_gt25|int}} reached 25 in the province mean. All {{a4.warnings_summary.sent|int}} warnings were logged between 30 September and 31 October (Bangkok time), after the bounded correction of Table~\ref{tab:incidents}, and the {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} province-days fall on only {{a4.warnings_unique.distinct_target_days|int}} target dates, so the interval above, which treats them as independent, is optimistic. We have not tested why they over-called.

\subsection{Whose fire is it?}
In the 14 days from 21 October the detection feed held {{a8.region_detections_all|int}} detections, but {{derived.outside_box_share|pct0}} of them fell outside the box the production smoke indicator looks at, mostly in Indonesia, which the feed has covered since 19 October. In the production box (4--29$^\circ$N, 92--110$^\circ$E: mainland Southeast Asia and its neighbours) there were {{a8.box_detections|int}} detections, {{a8.inside_thailand|int}} of them inside Thailand. Three satellites often see the same fire, so we count 0.25$^\circ$ cells per day: {{derived.outside_cell_share|pct0}} of the {{a8.cell_days_total|int}} fire cell-days lay outside Thailand (95\% CI over days {{derived.outside_cell_ci0 .. derived.outside_cell_ci1|pct0}}), and {{a8.frp_outside_share|pct0}} of the fire radiative power ({{a8.frp_outside_ci_day_bootstrap[0] .. a8.frp_outside_ci_day_bootstrap[1]|pct0}}) (Fig.~\ref{fig:geo}b). The box also holds Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam, southern China, Peninsular Malaysia and Myanmar, so ``outside'' is not ``Myanmar''. On {{a8.active_share|pct0}} of {{a8.province_days|int}} province-days the weighted source index reached 20 units. The foreign-share calculation uses unweighted cell radiative-power proxies allocated by within-cell detection counts; it is not the foreign fraction of the distance-weighted index, a measured cross-border power total, or an attribution of Thai PM. Figure~\ref{fig:geo}c retains this exploratory allocation with that limitation explicit.

Does the indicator predict anything? Across provinces and days, next-day \pmtf{} rose with the log of the weighted source index after removing each province's mean and each day's mean ($\rho={{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.rho_within_province|f2}}$; 95\% CI over days {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.ci_cluster_bootstrap[0] .. a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.ci_cluster_bootstrap[1]|f2}}; {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.pairs|int}} province-days in {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.provinces|int}} provinces, but only {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_log.days|int}} days). In the eight upper-north provinces, where the claim matters most, it is $\rho={{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_upper_north.rho|f2}}$ (CI {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_upper_north.ci[0] .. a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_upper_north.ci[1]|f2}}); elsewhere $\rho={{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_rest.rho|f2}}$ (CI {{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_rest.ci[0] .. a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_rest.ci[1]|f2}}). The result is not stable. Centring on the province alone gives $\rho={{a8.next_day_pm25_vs_upwind_upper_north.rho_province_centred_only|f2}}$ in the upper north, and eight provinces that share one wind are not eight independent tests. Nine days cannot settle it, and the window sits before the season in which it matters. We therefore do not claim that the indicator forecasts transported smoke; it describes where smoke could come from.

The domestic picture is steadier, for one kind of burning. The burn-scar product covers agricultural fires (rice, sugar cane and maize) in {{a7.provinces|int}} provinces over three seasons; it does not map forest or grass fires. Agricultural burning is concentrated: ten provinces hold {{a7.concentration.2024.top10_share|pct0}}, {{a7.concentration.2025.top10_share|pct0}} and {{a7.concentration.2026.top10_share|pct0}} of the burned area in 2024, 2025 and 2026, and the ranking barely moves between years ($\rho={{a7.rank_stability.2024_vs_2026.rho|f2}}$ for 2024 against 2026; CI {{a7.rank_stability_ci_2024_2026[0] .. a7.rank_stability_ci_2024_2026[1]|f2}}); {{a7.top10_overlap.all_three|int}} of the ten are the same in all three years (Fig.~\ref{fig:geo}a). The upper north holds only {{a7.upper_north_share_jan_apr.2024|pct0}}--{{a7.upper_north_share_jan_apr.2026|pct0}} of the agricultural burned area of January to April, and there the crop is mostly maize ({{a7.crop_mix_upper_north.corn|pct0}}), where elsewhere it is paddy and sugar cane. How much of the upper north's smoke comes from forest fire instead, these maps cannot say. Agricultural burned area in January to April 2024, a season that followed an El Ni\~no (the index stood at {{a10.enso.seasons.2024.DJF|sf2}} in December--February, NOAA), was {{a7.jan_apr_km2.2024|int}}~km\textsuperscript{2}, {{derived.burn_2024_over_2025|f1}} times that of 2025 ({{a7.jan_apr_km2.2025|int}}~km\textsuperscript{2}); with three seasons, that is a hint and not a test.

\subsection{What cameras and airports can see}
Public cameras are cheap and numerous, and of little use for this question so far. Of {{derived.frames_total|int}} frames from {{a5.cameras|int}} cameras, {{derived.low_light_share|pct0}} were taken in low light and {{derived.judged_share|pct0}} could be judged against a camera's own clear-day baseline. Across {{a5.paired_frames|int}} frames from {{a5.paired_cameras|int}} cameras with a ground station nearby, contrast loss rose with \pmtf{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:optical}a) but the correlation is weak: $\rho={{a5.spearman_contrast_loss_vs_pm25.rho|f2}}$ (CI {{a5.spearman_contrast_loss_vs_pm25.ci_cluster_bootstrap_by_camera[0] .. a5.spearman_contrast_loss_vs_pm25.ci_cluster_bootstrap_by_camera[1]|f2}}), and within a single camera over time the median $\rho$ is {{a5.within_camera_rho.median|f2}}. As an alarm, scored against the hourly \pmtf{} at the nearest station, the ``possible haze'' flag has specificity of {{a5.flag.25.0.specificity|pct0}} and sensitivity of {{a5.flag.25.0.sensitivity|pct0}} for frames at 25 or above, and precision of {{a5.flag.25.0.ppv|pct0}} (Fig.~\ref{fig:optical}c). Of the {{a5.possible_haze_with_rh|int}} flagged frames with a humidity reading, {{a5.possible_haze_rh_ge90_share|pct0}} were at 90\% relative humidity or above, where fog is the likelier cause; a 1-in-25 sample of {{a5.unchanged_rh_n|int}} unflagged frames gave {{a5.unchanged_rh_ge90_share|pct0}}, so humidity does not separate them.

Airport visibility fares no better. METAR visibility is censored at 10~km in this dataset: {{a6.share_at_ceiling_ge9_9|pct0}} of hours provide only a lower bound on range, limiting resolution of cleaner conditions. Below the ceiling, visibility and \pmtf{} are weakly related, and with the opposite sign to the simple expectation that visibility decreases as particle extinction increases ($\rho={{a6.spearman_vis_vs_pm25_below_ceiling.rho|sf2}}$; CI {{a6.spearman_vis_vs_pm25_below_ceiling.ci_cluster_bootstrap_by_airport[0] .. a6.spearman_vis_vs_pm25_below_ceiling.ci_cluster_bootstrap_by_airport[1]|f2}}; {{a6.pair_airports|int}} airports). In the 25--37.5 band, {{a6.p_reduced_by_band[3].share|pct0}} of hours had visibility under 10~km against about 20\% in the cleaner bands, but that rests on {{a6.p_reduced_by_band[3].n|int}} hours at {{a6.p_reduced_by_band_cluster[3].airports|int}} airports (cluster CI {{a6.p_reduced_by_band_cluster[3].ci[0] .. a6.p_reduced_by_band_cluster[3].ci[1]|pct0}}), {{a6.p_reduced_by_band_cluster[3].largest_airport_hour_share|pct0}} of them from a single airport; without it the share is {{a6.p_reduced_by_band_cluster[3].share_without_largest|pct0}} (Fig.~\ref{fig:optical}b). In {{a6.metar_hours|int}} airport-hours the observers' weather codes never once attributed reduced visibility to haze or smoke, but those codes are normally reported only at low visibility and just {{a6.share_below_5km|pct1}} of hours were below 5~km. The test needs the burning season.

\subsection{Alerts people would see}
The alert log holds {{a10.alerts.alert_rules_all.pm25_level|int}} level alerts: {{a10.alerts.pm25_level_by_source.air4thai|int}} from PCD-hosted snapshots and {{a10.alerts.pm25_level_by_source.gistda_pm25|int}} from GISTDA. Among {{a10.alerts.air4thai.evaluable|int}} PCD and {{a10.alerts.gistda_pm25.evaluable|int}} GISTDA alerts with at least four available snapshots in the alert-hour-plus-five-hour window, {{a10.alerts.air4thai.persistent_ge3h_of_6.share|pct0}} and {{a10.alerts.gistda_pm25.persistent_ge3h_of_6.share|pct0}}, respectively, had at least three snapshots at or above 37.5. This window includes the alert hour, not six wholly subsequent hours. The earlier daily-hit percentages are withdrawn: missing complete-day outcomes were counted as non-hits, and a same-calendar-day mean includes time before the alert. These outcomes need separate denominators and cannot establish prospective daily warning accuracy. Neighbour corroboration was {{a10.alerts.air4thai.corroborated_by_neighbor_ge25.share|pct0}} among {{a10.alerts.air4thai.corroborated_by_neighbor_ge25.n|int}} alerts with sufficient neighbours; {{a10.alerts.air4thai.top5_share|pct0}} of PCD alerts came from five stations. Repeated alerts and shared stations are not independent evidence, and user fatigue was not measured.

\subsection{The year it arrives in}
The Oceanic Ni\~no Index for August to October 2026 is {{a10.enso.latest.anom|sf2}}, the highest of the {{a10.enso.n_ASO_years|int}} August--October seasons in NOAA's record, above 1997 and 2015. Mainland Southeast Asia responds less to El Ni\~no than the Maritime Continent does \citep{nurdiati2022enso}, and we make no forecast of the coming season from it. We note it because the proposed tests below will be read in a year that is unusual on one dimension.

