\section{Discussion}
The audit identifies integration and verification problems within this prototype. It does not establish that agencies fail to coordinate or that the system improves public response.

\emph{Coordination.} The integrated products have different spatial support and do not always agree. Two official sources assign a province to different air-quality bands on {{derived.category_differ|pct0}} of province-days; the ground network puts {{a9.stations_per_province.bangkok_share|pct0}} of its stations in one city and {{derived.upper_north_stations|int}} in the eight provinces the dust season hits hardest; seventeen streams (sixteen public feeds and one sister system) arrive through eight kinds of interface from more than ten organisations, two of them gated by a free account. None of this is a failure of any one agency. This audit does not establish the institutional causes of those differences. A small team could do the join, using published interfaces under their access conditions. That is the argument for the approach, and it is also its limit: a join can show the disagreement but cannot say which source is right.

\emph{Responsiveness.} Speed is not the bottleneck; judgement is. Snapshots typically reach the collector in about half an hour; alert acceptance and delivery have not been verified. But the alert rule fires on hours, people respond to days, and {{derived.warned_overcalled|int}} of {{a4.warnings_unique.evaluable|int}} forecast warnings did not exceed the numerical benchmark at any station's derived daily mean. Alert fatigue is a documented cost \citep{saberian2017alerts}, and an alert that is quick and often unconfirmed invites it. Table~\ref{tab:tests} proposes testing more selective alerts; the effect on people remains unknown.

\emph{The chain.} The slowest measured link is the first, the fire detection ({{a3.firms_lag_min.median|min2h}} hours at the median), and much of that delay is our own six-hour polling, so the first fix is ours. The upwind indicator turned out to be what we said it was, a map of where smoke can come from, and not yet a forecast. The most useful thing in the chain may be the least glamorous: a dated, source-tagged statement that {{derived.outside_cell_share|pct0}} of the fire cell-days on the mainland in a fortnight lay outside Thailand.

\emph{Bypassing, honestly.} What did bypassing the bureaucracy buy? It bought speed (a working chain in one season, by one author with AI assistants and one laptop), implementation simplicity (no declared server npm dependencies; vendored libraries, hardware, hosting and labour still have costs, which were not evaluated) and a record of its own failures that nobody had to approve. It did not buy the sensors that are missing, the agreement between sources, or the registration form for a free token. Nor does it touch the diplomatic layer. Satellites can say that a fire burns in a particular place; they cannot say who lit it, and we make no claim of attribution. What an open record can do for diplomacy is modest: it offers a shared set of numbers on which an argument could start. The treaty rests on each party's own policies for its own territory \citep{aathp2002text}, and whether such a record changes anything is not something this paper tests.

\emph{What we would ask of others.} These are proposals that follow from the audit, not findings. (i)~Agree one published value per province, or publish the difference between sources with the reason. (ii)~Fill the upper-north gap with calibrated low-cost sensors \citep{barkjohn2021purpleair,dejchanchaiwong2023lcs} whose record is published beside the regulatory one. (iii)~Pair every \pmtf{} station with a \pmten{} reading. (iv)~Warn on persistence, not on the hour. (v)~Publish the age of every fire detection beside it. (vi)~Say what happens to a free-token source that has no token. None needs a treaty.

\section{Limitations}
The ground record is {{a1.window.days|int}} days long and ends before the dust season, so we cannot say how the system behaves in the conditions it was built for. Most conclusions about burning rest on a 14-day window of fire detections and on three seasons of burn scars; the one forecast we could verify, CAMS, is the only model we tested, and we did not run trajectory models \citep{stein2015hysplit}. The correction, the thresholds and the weights of the score were set by the authors and not fitted to health outcomes, which we did not study. Station-day means rest on the PCD feed as served, and while we screened out values of 500~\pmu{} or more, we did not validate that exclusion or audit calibration. The verification sample for high events is small: {{a4.leads.0.events.37.5.events_observed|int}} province-days above 37.5. The push channel was exercised with one or two subscribed chats, and the broadcast-timing proxy does not establish server acceptance or delivery to any person. Many of the intervals use Wilson bounds that treat events as independent when they cluster in time, and the fire and transport results rest on 14 days, of which nine have a next-day record. The airport data were collected before the visibility station table was rebuilt, when 3 of 28 codes were mislabelled. Camera and visibility analyses are pilots; their confidence intervals are wide and one airport dominates the visibility result. The system runs on one machine and its record contains its own gaps; five gaps of over six hours are unexplained. The code was largely written with AI assistants, which is a risk we reduced with tests ({{a10.system.test_files|int}} files at the frozen code census) and did not remove. We have not shown that the system changes anyone's behaviour or health, or that people can use it right away beyond a handful of test users. Finally, the audit was prepared by the system's developers; the tests above, and the public code, exist so that others can check it.

